# DUET

## English

DUET is a modified Linux 6.14.6 kernel source tree for studying low-latency guarantees in block I/O scheduling. The core DUET logic is built around the BFQ I/O scheduler, block-device queue byte thresholds, and burst I/O tagging.

Compared with the official Linux v6.14.6 source tree, excluding test scripts and the CPU monitoring extension, DUET adds about 373 effective lines of kernel code, deletes 4 effective lines, and changes about 377 effective lines in total. Blank lines and comments are not counted.

The CPU-usage-based revocation mechanism is an additional protection experiment for malicious burst-tagged processes. It is used to evaluate robustness under untrusted tagging, but it is not part of DUET's core scheduling logic.

This repository can be built and booted like a regular Linux kernel source tree. The `test/` directory contains functional and performance test scripts. The `experiments/` directory contains experiment data, plotting scripts, and paper figures.

### How to Run

GCC 14 is recommended. GCC 15 may fail to build Linux 6.14.6 because of stricter compiler checks.

Install common dependencies:

```bash
sudo apt install build-essential gcc-14 g++-14 flex bison libssl-dev libelf-dev bc pahole libncurses-dev libaio-dev fio
```

Build the kernel:

```bash
make CC=gcc-14 x86_64_defconfig
make CC=gcc-14 -j"$(nproc)"
```

Install modules and the kernel:

```bash
sudo make modules_install
sudo make install
```

Update the boot menu and reboot:

```bash
sudo update-grub
sudo reboot
```

After rebooting, check the active kernel:

```bash
uname -r
```

If the configuration is active, the kernel version should contain `duet`.

### Test Scripts

Test scripts are located in `test/`. Most scripts assume `/dev/nvme0n1` as the target device. Before running experiments, update `TEST_DEV` or the device path in the scripts for your machine. Some scripts change the block scheduler, drop caches, run fio/RocksDB, and require `sudo`.

The scripts below are organized according to the experiment order in Section 4 of the paper.

```text
4.2 DUET threshold selection, corresponding to Figure 7, Figure 8, and Figure 9
```

Main scripts: `test/lowthreshold_clean.sh`, `test/dual.sh`.

`lowthreshold_clean.sh` runs batch tests for none, BFQ, DUET same-threshold, and DUET dual-threshold configurations. It generates sustained fio workloads, sweeps background worker counts, I/O sizes, and high/low thresholds, and records sustained throughput and burst latency.

`dual.sh` is used for high/low threshold combination experiments. It focuses on how `T_low` affects burst latency and sustained throughput when `T_high` is fixed or varied.

```text
4.3 Sustained workload I/O size sensitivity, corresponding to Figure 10 and Figure 11
```

Main scripts: `test/auto_test.sh`, `test/human_friendly.sh`.

`auto_test.sh` is the basic batch experiment driver. It runs sustained and burst workloads under none, BFQ, and DUET, and records latency and throughput under different sustained I/O sizes.

`human_friendly.sh` is a more interactive version for manually checking the experiment flow, device setup, result directories, and logs.

Figure 11 uses the same testing logic on another SSD and the corresponding data file.

```text
4.4 Sustained workload parallelism sensitivity, corresponding to Figure 12
```

Main scripts: `test/auto_test.sh`, `test/human_friendly.sh`.

This experiment fixes the sustained I/O size and changes the number of background sustained workers. It evaluates how DUET improves burst latency under different sustained workload parallelism levels and whether sustained throughput remains stable.

```text
4.5 Burst workload I/O size sensitivity, corresponding to Figure 13
```

Main scripts: `test/old_test.sh`, `test/auto_test.sh`.

`old_test.sh` keeps an earlier burst-parameter sweep, including burst read/write type, burst block size, and burst iodepth. It is useful for reproducing the sensitivity of burst latency and throughput to burst I/O size.

```text
4.6 Burst workload parallelism sensitivity, corresponding to Figure 14
```

Main scripts: `test/old_test.sh`, `test/record.sh`.

This experiment changes the number of concurrently running burst workers and observes whether DUET keeps burst latency low while recording sustained throughput changes. `record.sh` is a lightweight helper for saving experiment output.

```text
4.7 RocksDB application-level evaluation, corresponding to Figure 15 and Figure 16
```

Main script: `test/rocksdb_test.sh`.

`rocksdb_test.sh` uses fio as the background sustained workload and RocksDB `db_bench` as the foreground burst-like workload. It compares none, BFQ, and DUET, and records fio throughput and RocksDB `Get` latency. Figure 15 corresponds to different sustained parallelism levels. Figure 16 corresponds to different DUET low-threshold settings.

The modified RocksDB source used in this experiment is available at:

```text
https://github.com/didudidu-lulu/rocksdb-interval-time
```

```text
4.8 CPU-side burst-privilege monitoring, corresponding to Table 4, Figure 17, and Figure 18
```

Main scripts: `test/exp_a_override_revoke.sh`, `test/exp_cs_monitor_overhead_manyproc.sh`, `test/exp_ioprio_monitor_hotpath.sh`.

`exp_a_override_revoke.sh` is the main CPU-monitoring functionality test. It runs three scenarios: a fair baseline, a malicious process with `ioprio_override` while CPU monitoring is disabled, and a malicious process with `ioprio_override` while CPU monitoring is enabled. It verifies whether the kernel revokes the malicious burst tag and restores legitimate sustained fio throughput.

`exp_cs_monitor_overhead_manyproc.sh` uses `perf bench sched messaging` to create many processes and repeatedly applies `ioprio_override` to them. It evaluates the runtime overhead of CPU monitoring on context switching and system execution.

`exp_ioprio_monitor_hotpath.sh` uses ftrace to observe `ioprio_ov_cpu_work_fn`, including the number of worker invocations, per-round cost, and scaling behavior as the number of monitored processes grows.

Common helper: `test/exp_common.sh`. It contains shared functions for building test tools, configuring BFQ and thresholds, toggling the CPU-monitoring sysctl, generating fio job files, watching queue bytes, and parsing results.

Test program sources:

```text
bdev_set_bytes.c          calls the bdev_set_bytes syscall
bdev_get_bytes.c          calls the bdev_get_bytes syscall
ioprio_override.c         calls the ioprio_override syscall
burst_task.c              generates burst I/O workloads
malicious_sustained.c     generates sustained I/O plus CPU activity for monitor-revocation tests
```

Build the test tools:

```bash
cd test
source ./exp_common.sh
exp_build_tools
```

